---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Section from '../../../components/Section.astro';
import Eyebrow from '../../../components/Eyebrow.astro';
import Button from '../../../components/Button.astro';
import Icon from '../../../components/Icon.astro';
import PageHero from '../../../components/PageHero.astro';
import Photo from '../../../components/Photo.astro';
import PlanPicker from '../../../components/PlanPicker.astro';
import Reviews from '../../../components/Reviews.astro';
import FAQ from '../../../components/FAQ.astro';
import EnquiryForm from '../../../components/EnquiryForm.astro';
import { nap, site, ratings, homecareReviews } from '../../../data/site.ts';
import { homecareLpFaqs } from '../../../data/faqs.ts';

/* =====================================================================
   /lp/homecare/ — Abbey Home Care paid-traffic landing page.
   Built to `Landing pages/abbey-homecare-lp-spec.md`, 3 August 2026.

   WHY THIS IS NOT A TRIMMED `/homecare-plans/`. That page is built for MOST
   AWARE traffic: someone who searched "boiler cover Whitby" and is choosing a
   supplier, for whom opening with the category, the price and a pricing table is
   correct. Meta traffic is PROBLEM AWARE at best — they were not thinking about
   their boiler at all. So the top third is materially different (the problem is
   agitated before any price appears) and the pricing table has gone entirely:
   the three plan cards carry what a cold visitor needs, and the full seven-tier
   matrix is dead weight here.

   NOINDEX, AND THAT IS THE POINT. This page and `/homecare-plans/` target the
   same terms. The site page carries Service + FAQPage + BreadcrumbList schema and
   is built to rank; two near-identical pages would cannibalise each other. So
   this one is `noindex`, excluded from the sitemap in `astro.config.mjs`, and
   carries NO page-level schema — only the Organization + WebSite entities every
   page gets from BaseLayout. Deliberate, not an omission.

   ONE PAGE, ONE GOAL. `chromeless` removes the site header, footer and plan
   prompt. Every exit ramp on the site page has gone with them: no nav, no menu,
   no service links, no social icons, no areas list, and neither of the two
   closing cross-links to Plumbing & Heating and About. The second of those raised
   a real question ("who am I dealing with?") and the answer is now section 7 of
   this page rather than a link off it.

   WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, all recorded in LAUNCH.md:
   - NO TRACKING. GA4, the Meta Pixel + CAPI and the Ads conversion tag all fire
     from here and from /thank-you/, but no measurement IDs exist (Outstanding B2)
     and a pixel cannot ship without the consent banner, Consent Mode v2 and its
     entry in `src/data/consent-registry.ts` in the SAME change — that registry
     generates the cookie policy table, so a pixel added without it publishes a
     policy that is untrue. Launch blocker.
   - NO OFTEC BADGE OR CLAIM. The spec asks for one; OFTEC registration is
     UNCONFIRMED (Outstanding C3 — it comes from a sticker visible on a van in the
     About photograph), and no badge art exists in the repo either. Golden rule 1
     and standing rule 21.
   - NO GAS SAFE BADGE IMAGE, because the file does not exist. The registration
     number is confirmed and checkable, so it is stated as text in section 7.
   - NO DEADLINE ON THE SIGN-UP OFFER, and NO URGENCY SECTION AT ALL. The spec
     asked for both. Abbey have agreed no deadline, and inventing an expiry would
     wreck the tone the page runs on. The urgency section that stood in for it was
     cut on 3 August 2026 (Josh: "I have no idea what this is meant to be... think
     we just get rid") — it existed to satisfy a checklist item rather than because
     the page needed it, and its one useful fact, the 14-day claims wait, is
     answered properly in the FAQ. See the note where that section used to be.
   - THE OFFER IS PRESENTED AS A CHOICE, NOT A DISCOUNT (see `_Specs/11
     HOMECARE OFFER STRATEGY.md`). Service Care is simply £9.99 a month and
     includes the annual service, this one and every one after, no separate
     joining charge. Section 6 below presents that as a one-off-vs-plan
     choice, not a discount.

   "ONE FREE CALL-OUT", NOT "1 hour free call-out". The spec makes this a launch
   blocker; Josh has now ruled on it three times, most recently 3 August 2026:
   in Abbey's business the two phrasings describe the same entitlement, and the
   shorter one reads better. Angela's verified review in section 3 happens to use
   the customer's own version of it ("one callout per year").

   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   ⚠️ MESSAGE MATCH IS STILL OPEN. DO NOT LAUNCH WITHOUT WORKING THIS THROUGH.
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   Three checklist items cannot be scored until the Meta ad creative exists, and
   they are worth real points. The process rule is: BUILD THE AD FIRST, THEN MIRROR
   IT HERE, not the other way round. When the creative lands, check all three:

     1. HERO IMAGE — is it the same shot as the winning ad, or an obvious sibling
        from the same set? Right now it is `boiler-service-portrait.webp`, which was
        chosen for `/homecare-plans/` and a completely different (search-intent)
        audience. **It will very likely need replacing.**
     2. H1 — does it echo the ad's primary hook, in the words the visitor just read?
     3. OFFER — is the £9.99, and anything else the ad promises, above the fold?

   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   ⚠️ THIS PAGE IS FOR META TRAFFIC. DO NOT POINT GOOGLE SEARCH ADS AT IT.
   ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
   The H1 is a problem-agitation question, which is correct for Problem Aware
   traffic and is a sanctioned headline type. It carries no keyword, so against a
   search ad group it fails message match outright and would drag Quality Score and
   raise CPC. Search needs its own variant with a keyword-led H1 (see
   `_Specs/07 AD LANDING PAGE ALIGNMENT.md`, which lists the candidate ad groups).
   The eyebrow puts "Boiler cover in Whitby" in the hero regardless, but an eyebrow
   is not a headline and does not close that gap.
   ===================================================================== */

const title = 'Whitby Boiler Cover from £9.99 a Month | Abbey Group';
const description =
  'Local, Gas Safe boiler cover in Whitby from £9.99 a month. The annual service, safety certificate and priority response from the same team every year.';

/* ---- Section 2: without cover / with cover ----
   Two columns, three rows. An even grid, not a wrapping row (standing rule 12). */
const contrast = [
  { without: 'Ring round for whoever happens to be free', with: 'One number, and a team who already know your boiler' },
  { without: 'Pay whatever the call-out comes to', with: 'Covered, with priority response' },
  { without: 'Remember to book the service yourself', with: 'Booked in for you, every year' },
];

/* ---- Section 3: the annual visit ----
   The same six items as the site page, pushed one level further: the site page
   states the functional benefit and stops, and misses the personal one, which is
   the half that actually sells boiler cover. */
const includes = [
  { icon: 'wrench', t: 'Full boiler service', d: 'A complete service by a Gas Safe engineer, so small faults get caught while they are still small and cheap.' },
  { icon: 'shield-check', t: 'Gas safety certificate', d: 'Issued on the visit, so you are not lying awake wondering whether the thing in the cupboard is safe with kids in the house.' },
  { icon: 'gauge', t: 'Flue gas analysis', d: 'We check the boiler is burning cleanly and safely.' },
  { icon: 'filter', t: 'Heating filter cleaned', d: 'Keeps sludge out of the system, so the boiler is not working harder than it needs to and costing you more to run.' },
  { icon: 'thermometer', t: 'Balance of radiators', d: 'So every room heats evenly, not just the ones nearest the boiler.' },
  { icon: 'activity', t: 'Heating health check', d: 'A look over the whole system while we are there, so nothing gets missed.' },
];

/* ---- Section 5: the plans ----
   The three domestic tiers, priced from `02 PLAN DATA`. CTAs are first-person and
   name the value rather than the action ("Choose This Plan" on the site page is
   neither), and each carries the risk-removal line the site cards lack.

   `note` on Service Care+ states the ten-year eligibility rule in plain sight.
   It is already the gate the plan picker runs on (brochure T&C 2.1(a)(vi) and
   2.5(e)) but appears in no copy anywhere on the site, so today a visitor can be
   routed away from parts cover without ever being told why. */
const plans = [
  {
    name: 'Service Care',
    price: '£9.99',
    popular: false,
    blurb: 'Your full annual boiler service, gas safety certificate and heating health check, with one free call-out and priority response. The honest plan that keeps your boiler right.',
    cta: 'Start My Service Care',
    micro: 'Cancel any time. No cancellation fee.',
    note: null as string | null,
  },
  {
    name: 'Service Care+',
    price: '£19.99',
    popular: true,
    blurb: 'Everything in Service Care, plus unlimited call-outs and boiler parts and labour included. If the boiler needs a part, it is covered.',
    cta: 'Start My Service Care+',
    micro: 'For boilers under 10 years old. Cancel any time.',
    note: 'Parts cover needs a boiler under ten years old. If yours is older, Service Care is the plan available to you, and it still covers the service, the certificate and a call-out.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Ultimate Home Care',
    price: '£34.99',
    popular: false,
    blurb: 'Everything in Service Care+, plus central heating and plumbing parts and labour included, and 20% off any other work. The whole home looked after.',
    cta: 'Start My Ultimate Cover',
    micro: 'Everything covered. Cancel any time.',
    note: null,
  },
];

/* ---- Section 6: what Service Care buys every year, on £9.99 a month ---- */
const offerStack = [
  'The full annual service, booked in so you never have to remember it',
  'Your gas safety certificate issued on the visit',
  'Priority response when something goes wrong',
  'One free call-out',
  '5% off any other work you have done',
];

/* ---- Section 7: credentials, every one of them checkable ---- */
const credentials = [
  { t: `Gas Safe registered, number ${site.gasSafeNo}`, d: 'Look it up on the Gas Safe Register. Any gas work is carried out by a Gas Safe engineer, which is the law.' },
  { t: 'Working in Whitby since 2008', d: 'The same local firm many people here still know as Abbeygas.' },
  { t: `${nap.addressFull}`, d: 'A real office on Skinner Street you can walk into, not a call centre and not a PO box.' },
  { t: `${site.legalName}, company no. ${site.companyNo}`, d: `Registered in ${site.registeredIn}. Home Care Plans are service plans, not insurance.` },
];

/* ---- Section 8: reviews ----
   Curated down from the site page's six, per the spec:
   - Angela's is the strongest of the set ("came within hours when I had no
     heating") and it is now a pull quote in section 3, beside the
     priority-response claim it actually evidences, rather than sitting in a grid
     of six where it reads as one more card. It also removes the duplicate-Angela
     problem: "Angela" and "Angela Weldon" were both in the same block and read as
     the same person twice.
   - Karen Hartas's ("Very efficient and reliable service. Would certainly use
     again.") is cut. It is true and it is verified, but it makes no argument.
   Four remain, which the grid resolves to an even single row. */
const lpReviews = homecareReviews.filter(
  (r) => r.name !== 'Angela' && r.name !== 'Karen Hartas',
);
const priorityReview = homecareReviews.find((r) => r.name === 'Angela')!;
---
<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  noindex={true}
  chromeless={true}
  division="homecare"
>
  <!-- 1. HERO — "is this about me?"
       Problem-agitation, not category and price.

       THE EYEBROW REPLACES THE DIVISION MARKER, and it is the one structural
       departure from the site convention on this page. Every interior page carries
       a typographic marker reading "ABBEY / HOME CARE", because the header lockup
       always says ABBEY GROUP and something has to say which trade you are in
       (30 July 2026). That is the wrong job here: the LP Quality Checklist asks the
       value proposition to be clear inside five seconds and without industry
       knowledge, and "ABBEY HOME CARE" tells a cold visitor nothing.

       So the marker is off (`badge={false}`) and the eyebrow carries category,
       location and price instead — the five-second test passes before the visitor
       reaches the H1, and the H1 is left free to do the agitation the traffic
       actually needs. Both cannot be shown: they are two lines of ~12px caps in
       the same slot, which is clutter and a repeat of the same screen region
       (standing rule 14). The sub-brand is not lost — the bar reads ABBEY GROUP
       and the contrast table below names Abbey Home Care.

       It also puts the primary keyword in the hero, which is worth something if
       this page is ever pointed at search traffic. See the warning at the foot of
       this file: it is not built for that, and should not be reused for it.

       MESSAGE MATCH IS NOT YET SCORABLE. The LP Quality Checklist requires the
       hero visual and headline to mirror the winning ad creative for Meta and
       Display traffic, and no ad creative exists yet. `boiler-service-portrait`
       is the only boiler photograph in the set and is a reasonable placeholder;
       swap it, and the H1 if needed, once the ad is built. -->
  <PageHero
    division="homecare"
    badge={false}
    eyebrow="Boiler cover in Whitby · from £9.99 a month"
    title="When Your Boiler Packs In, Who's Actually Coming?"
    lead="With a national provider you get a call centre and a date in the diary. With us you get a Whitby team, priority response, and the same engineers looking after your boiler year after year. Cover from £9.99 a month."
    trust={['Gas Safe registered', 'Working in Whitby since 2008', `${ratings.google.value} from ${ratings.google.count} Google reviews`, 'Cancel any time']}
    image="/images/boiler-service-portrait.webp"
    {/* `sizes` is MEASURED, not estimated. The hero photo slot is a consistent
        ~80vw up to 700px, then capped at 360px, then 440px. An earlier guess of
        92vw was close enough to look right and wrong enough to be useless: at a
        390px viewport it asked for 718w, which is over the 700w candidate, so
        every DPR-2 phone still downloaded the 900w file and the srcset did
        nothing. Re-measure this if the hero layout changes. */}
    imageSrcset="/images/boiler-service-portrait-700.webp 700w, /images/boiler-service-portrait.webp 900w"
    imageSizes="(max-width: 700px) 80vw, (max-width: 900px) 360px, 440px"
    imageAlt="Abbey Group engineer servicing a boiler in a Whitby home"
  >
    <Button href="#which-plan" size="lg">Find My Plan in 3 Questions</Button>
    <Button href={`tel:${nap.phone.tel}`} variant="inverse-outline" size="lg">Call the Whitby Office</Button>
  </PageHero>

  <!-- 2. COST OF INACTION — "why should I care?"
       This section does not exist on the site page and is the main reason cold
       traffic converts here: a Problem Aware visitor needs the problem made real
       before a price means anything.

       EVERY FIGURE IS SOURCED. £120 to £230 is Abbey's own approved copy for an
       emergency call-out in this area (`plumbingFaqs`); "most home insurance will
       not cover a breakdown" is approved copy too (`homecareFaqs`); £119.88 is
       twelve months of a confirmed £9.99. The spec's "a new boiler runs to
       several thousand pounds" is NOT stated, because no such figure is confirmed
       anywhere in the project files. Abbey's real average repair and call-out
       figures would strengthen this section — Outstanding C3. -->
  <Section bg="sunken">
    <div class="coi">
      <div class="coi__copy reveal">
        <Eyebrow>The part nobody plans for</Eyebrow>
        <h2 class="h2">The bill you have not budgeted for</h2>
        <p class="coi__lead">
          Boilers do not fail in June. They fail on the coldest weekend of the year, which is
          exactly when every engineer's diary is already full.
        </p>
        <p class="coi__body">
          An emergency call-out around here runs from about £120 to £230 before any parts, and
          most home insurance will not cover a breakdown at all. Service Care is £9.99 a month,
          which is £119.88 for a whole year, and it includes the annual service you are
          supposed to be having anyway.
        </p>
        <p class="coi__kicker">
          The point of cover is not really the money. It is that you already know who is coming.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="coi__table reveal">
        <div class="coi__head coi__head--without">Without cover</div>
        <div class="coi__head coi__head--with">With Abbey Home Care</div>
        {contrast.map((row) => (
          <>
            <div class="coi__cell coi__cell--without">
              <Icon name="x" size={16} />
              <span>{row.without}</span>
            </div>
            <div class="coi__cell coi__cell--with">
              <Icon name="check" size={16} />
              <span>{row.with}</span>
            </div>
          </>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!-- 3. WHAT COVER ACTUALLY GETS YOU — "how does this help me?" -->
  <Section bg="white">
    <div class="get__head reveal">
      <Eyebrow>What every plan includes</Eyebrow>
      <h2 class="h2">Six checks, one visit, every single year</h2>
      <p class="get__intro">
        Whichever plan you pick, the yearly service is the full job, not a token once-over.
        This is what an engineer actually does while they are in your house.
      </p>
    </div>
    <!-- Was a flat two-column list of six identical tick chips, which is the
         section doing most of the selling and the easiest one on the page to skim
         past (Josh, 3 August 2026: "visually very boring, not catching the eye").
         Now a 3x2 card grid, each item with ITS OWN glyph rather than a sixth
         identical check, so the six read as six different things at a glance
         instead of as one grey list. White cards on the sunken band, the same
         invert the review and FAQ cards use. Six divides by three, so the last
         row is never ragged (standing rule 12). -->
    <ul class="get__grid reveal">
      {includes.map((i, n) => (
        <li class="get__item">
          <span class="get__chip"><Icon name={i.icon} size={20} /></span>
          <span class="get__n tnum" aria-hidden="true">{String(n + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
          <h3 class="get__t">{i.t}</h3>
          <p class="get__d">{i.d}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>

    <div class="get__foot reveal">
      <p class="get__extra">
        You also get priority response when you need us, and money off any other work you have
        done.
      </p>
      <!-- The review sits HERE, next to the objection it answers, rather than in
           the block of six further down. "Came within hours when I had no heating"
           is the only evidence on the page that priority response is real, and it
           does nothing for that claim eight sections away from it. Verbatim from
           the verified master, exclamation marks and all (convention #8). -->
      <blockquote class="get__quote">
        <p>{priorityReview.quote}</p>
        <footer><span>{priorityReview.name}</span>, {priorityReview.source}</footer>
      </blockquote>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!-- 4. PLAN PICKER — "which one is mine?"
       Three taps is a far easier yes on cold traffic than "choose a subscription
       tier", and it self-segments homeowner / landlord / holiday-let, which is the
       split that matters commercially in Whitby.

       The three questions are UNCHANGED: they run on the client's own eligibility
       terms and they work. What is new is that the answers now travel with the
       enquiry — see `planPickerTags` on the form in section 11.

       The result is NOT gated behind the form (spec §4.4). The tool's "nothing is
       stored, there is nothing to submit" honesty is the reason it reads as help
       rather than a lead-capture trick, and putting a form in front of the answer
       would spend that for a handful of extra addresses. Show the answer, then
       offer to set it up. -->
  <Section bg="sunken" id="which-plan">
    <!-- The eyebrow used to read "Not sure which one" directly above an H2 reading
         "Not sure which one is yours?" — the same phrase twice in one screen
         (standing rule 15), and between them they never said what the thing below
         actually was (Josh, 3 August 2026). The heading is now an instruction, the
         eyebrow names the tool, and the lead says plainly what to do. -->
    <div class="which__head reveal">
      <Eyebrow>Plan finder</Eyebrow>
      <h2 class="h2">Answer three questions, see your plan</h2>
      <p class="which__intro">
        About ten seconds. We will tell you which plan actually fits your boiler and what it
        saves you on day one. Nothing is stored, and there is nothing to submit.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- The tool now sits in its own white panel with an accent rule along the top,
         so it reads as something to use rather than more page. That also fixes a
         real bug: `PlanPicker`'s result card has a SUNKEN background, which works on
         `/homecare-plans/` where the tool sits on a white section, but here the
         section is sunken and the card was the same colour as the band it sat on.
         The white panel restores the contrast the component was designed for. -->
    <div class="which__panel reveal">
      <!-- The old-boiler replacement route points at this page's own form rather
           than at Plumbing & Heating: it was the only link off the page, and it
           landed a paid click in the full site where it cannot be counted as a
           conversion. The message itself is unchanged.
           `ctaFirstPerson` matches these buttons to the plan cards below, so the
           same action is not offered in two different voices on one page. -->
      <PlanPicker
        altHref="#enquiry"
        altLabel="Ask us about a new boiler"
        ctaFirstPerson={true}
        directSignup={false}
      />
    </div>

    <p class="which__after reveal">
      <strong>Want us to set it up?</strong> Leave your details at the bottom of the page and we
      will get it started. Your three answers come through with them, so we already know which
      plan to talk about when we ring.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <!-- 5. THE PLANS — "what does it cost?"
       The full seven-tier comparison table from the site page is CUT: it is the
       right asset for someone comparing suppliers and too heavy for someone who
       was not thinking about their boiler five minutes ago. The cards carry it. -->
  <Section bg="white" id="plans">
    <div class="plans__head reveal">
      <Eyebrow tone="default">Choose your cover</Eyebrow>
      <h2 class="h2">Boiler cover from £9.99 a month</h2>
      <p class="plans__intro">
        Three plans, and all of them include the same proper annual service. The household-name
        national providers will happily cover your boiler too, for about double, from roughly
        £19 to £30 a month, and it is worth keeping an eye on what their cheap first-year deals
        become in year two. Ours is £9.99, this year, next year and the year after. Most people
        choose Service Care+, because it covers the parts as well as the labour.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ul class="plans__grid reveal">
      {plans.map((p) => (
        <li class:list={['plan', { 'plan--popular': p.popular }]}>
          {p.popular && <span class="plan__flag">Most popular</span>}
          <h3 class="plan__name">{p.name}</h3>
          <p class="plan__price"><span class="tnum">{p.price}</span><span class="plan__per">/month</span></p>
          <p class="plan__blurb">{p.blurb}</p>
          {p.note && <p class="plan__note">{p.note}</p>}
          <Button href="#enquiry" variant={p.popular ? 'primary' : 'outline'} block>{p.cta}</Button>
          <p class="plan__micro">{p.micro}</p>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <!-- 6. THE SIGN-UP OFFER — "can I start cheaply?"
       Retired as a discount (17 Aug 2026): there is no £48, no half price, and no
       separate joining charge. Service Care is £9.99 a month and simply includes
       the annual service, this one and every one after. Presented honestly here
       as a one-off-vs-plan choice.

       THE CTA POINTS AT THE FORM, not at the ServiceM8 booking link the site page
       uses. Two reasons: an off-site booking page is an exit from a page with one
       goal, and it cannot be counted as a conversion, which on paid traffic makes
       it invisible. The lead is captured here and the date is confirmed on the
       call back. Flagged for Josh. -->
  <Section bg="dark" bleed>
    <div class="offer">
      <img class="offer__mark" src="/brand/crescent/mark-homecare.svg" alt="" aria-hidden="true" width="360" height="378" />
      <div class="container offer__inner reveal">
        <div class="offer__copy">
          <Eyebrow tone="inverse">If you are booking a service anyway</Eyebrow>
          <h2 class="offer__h2">Already booking a service? Turn it into cover for good.</h2>
          <p class="offer__lead">
            The engineer is coming anyway. Start Service Care instead of paying for a one-off
            visit, and this service is included today, and every year after, for £9.99 a month.
          </p>
          <p class="offer__kicker">
            One breakdown call-out on its own can cost more than a whole year of cover. This way
            you are never caught out, and never paying for a service twice.
          </p>
          <Button href="#enquiry" size="lg">Book My Service and Start Covered</Button>
          <!-- The guarantee states duration, conditions AND the process. The
               mechanism is Abbey's own, from the approved plan FAQ: "you can cancel
               at any time by emailing or writing to us." NOT "a phone call", which
               the plan terms do not offer as a cancellation route, so promising it
               would be a claim the contract does not support. -->
          <p class="offer__risk">
            Leave your details and we will confirm the date when we ring you back. Stay because
            it is working, not because you are stuck: no minimum term, cancel any month you
            like, and a full refund if you change your mind within 14 days. Cancelling is one
            email to the office.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="offer__card">
          <div class="offer__row offer__row--was">
            <span class="offer__label">Boiler service, one-off</span>
            <span class="offer__num tnum">£96</span>
          </div>
          <div class="offer__row offer__row--now">
            <span class="offer__label">Service Care, from today</span>
            <span class="offer__num tnum">£9.99</span>
            <span class="offer__then">a month, this service included every year</span>
          </div>
          <p class="offer__stackhead">And every year you also get</p>
          <ul class="offer__stack">
            {offerStack.map((s) => (
              <li><Icon name="check" size={15} /><span>{s}</span></li>
            ))}
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!-- 7. TRUST — "who are you?"
       The site page mentions Gas Safe nine times and shows a face zero times,
       which is thin for a business asking a stranger for a monthly direct debit.
       This is also where the site page's "read our story on About" cross-link
       went: the question is real, so it gets answered here instead of being sent
       somewhere else at the moment of decision.

       Only James has a headshot; the other five have none (Outstanding C1.3), so
       the team is shown as they actually are, in the group-and-fleet photograph,
       with James named because his role is confirmed. No stock faces and no
       invented names. -->
  <Section bg="white">
    <div class="trust">
      <div class="trust__copy reveal">
        <Eyebrow>Who you are dealing with</Eyebrow>
        <h2 class="h2">A Whitby firm, not a call centre</h2>
        <p class="trust__lead">
          When you ring, someone in the Skinner Street office picks up. When the engineer turns
          up, it is one of the same handful of people every year, and they will remember the
          boiler before you have finished describing it.
        </p>
        <ul class="trust__list">
          {credentials.map((c) => (
            <li class="trust__item">
              <span class="trust__chip"><Icon name="badge-check" size={16} /></span>
              <div><strong>{c.t}.</strong> {c.d}</div>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div class="trust__media reveal">
        <Photo
          src="/images/about-team-group.webp"
          alt="The Abbey Group team with their vans in Whitby"
          ratio="4 / 3"
        />
        <figure class="trust__person">
          <img src="/images/about-james-eddon-headshot.webp" alt="James Eddon, Managing Director of Abbey Group" width="120" height="120" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
          <figcaption>
            <strong>James Eddon</strong>
            <span>Managing Director. Set the business up as Abbey Gas in 2008.</span>
          </figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!-- 8. SOCIAL PROOF — "what do others say?" -->
  <Reviews
    eyebrow="Why people stay"
    heading="Cover from people who pick up the phone"
    reviews={lpReviews}
    bg="sunken"
    readAllHref={ratings.googleReviewLink}
    readAllLabel="See our reviews on Google"
    readAllExternal={true}
  />

  <!-- THE PROOF BAND, rebuilt 3 August 2026.
       It was three small numbers on a thin white strip and it read as filler
       (Josh: "honestly just looks a bit shit"). Two changes:

       1. It is now a dark band with the division crescent behind it, so the score
          gets the same weight as the sign-up offer rather than sitting in a gap.
       2. THE FACEBOOK FIGURE IS GONE. Seven recommendations is not something to
          shout about (Josh), and putting it beside 19 Google reviews invited the
          comparison. The remaining two facts are the rating and the year, both of
          which stand on their own.

       No logo bar, because there is nothing honest to build one from: neither the
       Gas Safe nor the OFTEC badge file exists in the repo, and inventing brand art
       is standing rule 21. The visual hook is what we genuinely have — a single
       star glyph and the number set large. Note it is ONE star, not five: `Stars`
       renders whole stars only, and five filled stars next to "4.6" would be an
       embellishment (standing rule 6a).

       No AggregateRating here either: BaseLayout's Organization already carries the
       real 4.6 / 19 site-wide, and one entity gets one aggregate (rule 6b). -->
  <Section bg="dark" bleed>
    <div class="score">
      <img class="score__mark" src="/brand/crescent/mark-homecare.svg" alt="" aria-hidden="true" width="300" height="315" />
      <div class="container score__inner reveal">
        <div class="score__item">
          <span class="score__star" aria-hidden="true"><Icon name="star" size={26} /></span>
          <div>
            <p class="score__num tnum">{ratings.google.value}<span class="score__of">/5</span></p>
            <p class="score__label">from {ratings.google.count} Google reviews, all of them real jobs in and around Whitby</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="score__item">
          <span class="score__star" aria-hidden="true"><Icon name="calendar-check" size={26} /></span>
          <div>
            <p class="score__num tnum">2008</p>
            <p class="score__label">looking after boilers here since, first as Abbeygas and now as Abbey Group</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!-- 9. OBJECTION HANDLING — "what's the catch?"
       The four new questions lead: the ten-year rule, the exclusions, the 14-day
       claims wait and what actually happens after you get in touch. All four come
       from Abbey's own terms and none is answered anywhere on the site today. See
       the note in `src/data/faqs.ts`.

       One question the four cannot answer yet: whether there is an excess per
       claim. Nothing in the specs or the brochure says either way, and it is NOT
       safe to assume there is none — if there is one and the page does not say so,
       that is a complaint. Outstanding C3. -->
  <FAQ
    eyebrow="What is the catch"
    heading="The questions worth asking before you sign up"
    items={homecareLpFaqs}
    bg="white"
  />

  <!-- 10. URGENCY — REMOVED 3 August 2026 (Josh: "I have no idea what this is
       meant to be. I think without this section it would make no difference. Think
       we just get rid.")

       He is right, and it is worth recording why rather than just deleting it. The
       section existed to satisfy a checklist item, not because the page needed it:
       its two facts were the 14-day claims wait and "diaries fill from October",
       and the first is already answered properly in the FAQ below ("How quickly can
       I claim after joining?"), where it also keeps the two separate fortnights
       straight. So nothing factual is lost, and a section whose only job was to
       score a point has gone.

       Consequence, stated plainly: the checklist's "urgency uses specific numbers
       and dates" item drops back to partly met. The computed live date went with
       the section. Do NOT reinstate it by inventing a deadline on the sign-up
       offer — Abbey have agreed none (Outstanding C3/D3). If they ever do agree
       one, that is the honest way to close this item. -->

  <!-- 11. FINAL CTA + FORM — "what do I do?"
       PHONE IS REQUIRED AND SECOND IN THE ORDER. The site forms capture name,
       email, house and postcode, on a page whose whole argument is that a real
       person answers when you call. For a local trade the phone number is the
       highest-value field and the fastest close.

       `redirect` sends a completed enquiry to /thank-you/, which is the URL an ad
       platform can count as a conversion. `planPickerTags` merges the quiz answers
       and the recommended plan into the lead, read from the DOM at submit and
       stored nowhere.

       ⚠️ THE FORM STILL GOES NOWHERE. `PUBLIC_GHL_WEBHOOK` is unset, so a
       submission validates, redirects and looks entirely successful while the lead
       is discarded. Paying for traffic into this is the worst possible failure
       mode, because nothing about it looks broken. Launch blocker 1. -->
  <EnquiryForm
    eyebrow="Last step"
    heading="Get covered, or just ask us a question"
    lead="Leave your details and we will ring you back to set it up or talk it through, whichever you need. There is no pressure and no script."
    source="lp-homecare-meta"
    tags="homecare,boiler-cover,lp,meta"
    submitLabel="Get Me Covered"
    bg="sunken"
    redirect="/thank-you/"
    planPickerTags={true}
    fields={[
      { name: 'name', label: 'Name', required: true, autocomplete: 'name' },
      { name: 'phone', label: 'Phone', type: 'tel', required: true, autocomplete: 'tel' },
      { name: 'email', label: 'Email', type: 'email', required: true, autocomplete: 'email' },
      { name: 'postcode', label: 'Postcode', autocomplete: 'postal-code' },
      { name: 'house', label: 'House name or number', autocomplete: 'address-line1' },
    ]}
  >
    <!-- Duration, conditions and process, so the guarantee is specific on all
         three counts. "One email" is the mechanism Abbey's own plan FAQ states. -->
    <ul class="ef__risk">
      <li><Icon name="check" size={15} /><span>Cancel any time, no minimum term</span></li>
      <li><Icon name="check" size={15} /><span>No cancellation fee</span></li>
      <li><Icon name="check" size={15} /><span>Full refund within 14 days</span></li>
      <li><Icon name="check" size={15} /><span>Cancelling is one email to the office</span></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="ef__talk">
      Prefer to talk? Call the Whitby office on
      <a class="tnum" href={`tel:${nap.phone.tel}`}>{nap.phone.label}</a>
      and a real person will answer.
    </p>
  </EnquiryForm>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .h2 { font-size: clamp(32px, 3.2vw, 46px); color: var(--text-heading); margin-top: 10px; }

  /* ---- 2. COST OF INACTION ---- */
  .coi { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 0.95fr; gap: 56px; align-items: start; }
  .coi__copy { display: flex; flex-direction: column; }
  .coi__lead { margin-top: 18px; font-size: var(--text-md); line-height: var(--leading-relaxed); color: var(--text-heading); font-weight: var(--weight-medium); }
  .coi__body { margin-top: 16px; font-size: var(--text-base); line-height: var(--leading-relaxed); color: var(--text-body); }
  .coi__kicker {
    margin-top: 22px;
    padding-left: 16px;
    border-left: 2px solid var(--page-accent, var(--orange-500));
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    line-height: var(--leading-relaxed);
    color: var(--text-heading);
  }

  /* Two columns, three rows, as an even grid. */
  .coi__table {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    background: var(--white);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    box-shadow: var(--shadow-sm);
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .coi__head {
    padding: 14px 18px;
    font-size: 12.5px;
    font-weight: var(--weight-bold);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
  }
  .coi__head--without { color: var(--text-muted); background: var(--surface-sunken); }
  .coi__head--with { color: var(--page-accent-ink, var(--orange-800)); background: var(--page-accent-soft, var(--orange-50)); }
  .coi__cell {
    display: flex;
    gap: 10px;
    align-items: flex-start;
    padding: 16px 18px;
    font-size: 14.5px;
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
  }
  .coi__cell--without { color: var(--text-muted); }
  .coi__cell--with { color: var(--text-heading); font-weight: var(--weight-medium); background: color-mix(in oklch, var(--page-accent-soft, var(--orange-50)) 45%, var(--white)); }
  .coi__cell--without :global(svg) { color: var(--ink-200); flex: none; margin-top: 1px; }
  .coi__cell--with :global(svg) { color: var(--page-accent-ink, var(--orange-800)); flex: none; margin-top: 1px; }
  .coi__table > :nth-last-child(1), .coi__table > :nth-last-child(2) { border-bottom: 0; }

  /* ---- 3. WHAT EVERY PLAN INCLUDES ---- */
  .get__head { max-width: 680px; margin-bottom: 40px; }
  .get__intro { margin-top: 16px; font-size: var(--text-base); line-height: var(--leading-relaxed); color: var(--text-body); }

  /* Cards, not a list: this section is what actually sells the plan, so it has to
     be scannable in one pass rather than read. */
  .get__grid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 18px; }
  .get__item {
    position: relative;
    /* Sunken cards on a white band: the same invert the Reviews and FAQ cards use,
       so a card always separates from whichever ground the section is set to. */
    background: var(--surface-sunken);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    padding: 26px 24px 24px;
  }
  .get__chip {
    display: inline-flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;
    width: 44px; height: 44px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-signature);
    /* Full-strength accent with a navy glyph: the one glyph colour legible on
       every division accent (standing rule 5), and far more visible than the 4%
       tint the flat list used. */
    background: var(--page-accent, var(--orange-500));
    color: var(--ink-500);
  }
  /* A quiet index, so the six read as a set you can count rather than six
     unrelated cards. Decorative, hence aria-hidden on the element. */
  .get__n {
    position: absolute;
    top: 24px; right: 24px;
    font-size: 15px;
    font-weight: var(--weight-bold);
    color: var(--border-default);
    line-height: 1;
  }
  .get__t {
    margin-top: 18px;
    font-size: 17.5px;
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    line-height: var(--leading-snug);
    color: var(--text-heading);
  }
  .get__d { margin-top: 8px; font-size: 14.5px; line-height: var(--leading-relaxed); color: var(--text-body); }

  .get__foot { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 0.9fr; gap: 40px; align-items: center; margin-top: 36px; }
  .get__extra { font-size: var(--text-base); line-height: var(--leading-relaxed); color: var(--text-body); }
  .get__quote {
    padding: 22px 24px;
    background: var(--surface-sunken);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--page-accent, var(--orange-500));
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius-md) var(--radius-md) 0;
  }
  .get__quote p { font-size: 15.5px; line-height: var(--leading-relaxed); color: var(--text-heading); }
  .get__quote footer { margin-top: 12px; font-size: var(--text-sm); color: var(--text-muted); }
  .get__quote footer span { font-weight: var(--weight-bold); color: var(--text-heading); }

  /* ---- 4. PLAN FINDER ---- */
  .which__head { max-width: 680px; margin-bottom: 32px; }
  .which__intro { margin-top: 16px; font-size: var(--text-base); line-height: var(--leading-relaxed); color: var(--text-body); }
  .which__panel {
    background: var(--white);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-subtle);
